Passage:
Athanasius of Alexandria
In his account of his first exile in 328, Athanasius mentions a theological difference between himself and Arsenius, without going into detail. After his fourth council had condemned Arius and deposed Arius from his post as presbyter at Alexandria, the participants sent a letter to Constantine, referring to Arius' views on eisophoria ("coming back to God"), saying that Arius wished to conduct himself in accordance with this in order to have access to God, and calling Arius "a contempter of the Father", while saying that Arius himself did not do so. Athanasius refers to this letter in his "Discourses Against the Arians", writing that the council tried to re-insert Arius into the church by other means, and when Arius was unwilling to do so, they expelled him permanently. Constantine decided that Arius should be reinstated, resulting in Athanasius' first exile. Arius did not become bishop, however. Athanasius had been told that Constantine, after 11 July 328, had been in contact with Arius, and that he had attempted to reconcile the two. The latter assertion was apparently true, as Constantine wrote to all the bishops of the church in 335 that "Arius, having been separated from his episcopal seat, [had] returned [to] his party. He was called to Rome and Milan, and there, under the lead of the aforesaid Constantius, not a small number of bishops decided to "receive back" Arius, since it is said that the bishop of Rome and others convinced him that he had not been in the hands of the so-called 'Arians'. This obviously doesn't seem to be the case, even if the cause of the separation was the bishops' behavior in the Easter controversy." There is no indication that Athanasius knew that Constantine had been communicating with Arius or that he made any requests on Arius' behalf, although this interpretation is not altogether far-fetched.
"The second reason ... which the aforesaid archimandrite and the holy and righteous Athanasius... has invented ... against me and my venerable father, ... is ... that the former having been separated from his episcopal seat, and the latter being separated from his rank as bishop, [they have sought] to re-insert both of us. Now, for Athanasius, this is supposed to be the act of just and ecclesiastical excommunication, as being the cause of our being put out of our ranks. This charge was laid down by him at Tyre ... and brought about my exile [both] from the Church and from the city of Alexandria... Because of this reason, and the accusations mentioned previously [of theft and misappropriation of church property], I was subjected to unjust accusation ... as though I had been guilty of the aforesaid charges... And so, Athanasius, ... excommunicated me ... accused me falsely ... and excommunicated me... [till] the great, benevolent, most Christian, and most glorious Emperor Constantine the Great, sent for me from the East. This happened in the second year of his reign [i.e. after his second half year of the first year of his reign]. He was the brother of Constantius the Emperor who presides over the Western provinces, and the father of the Emperor Constantius in the Western provinces. His was a very great, blessed and Christian reign, due to his [salvation-oriented] speeches, actions and practices. This man sent for me to his palace [the house] which is called Loodein ... Wherein I repented of the sayings and proceedings which I had imagined and acted on previously, and I lost no time in writing an epistle to Athanasius... to apologize and to beg his pardon for the sayings and the doings of my earlier madness... For the peace and prosperity of the Church ... and for the Holy Baptism that both of us had received, we mutually forgave each other... And in great gladness and joy in Christ, I came to my father the bishop Athanasius, to their house in Alexandria... After the Easter festivities, I rejoiced in communion with Athanasius, with many bishops and faithful. We celebrated together [the feast] of the Holy Easter. And, after all this great joy [we broke fellowship] because the truth about [the secret conversations] with the Emperor Constantine the Great never became known. Once I found out that a letter was sent to him about him by Athanasius, I reported all this to him. But because of the words and letters from the aforesaid bishops and his own sons, he reproached me, saying that he didn't know how to deal with me. And he refused my approaches [to be reconciled with me].
The letter written to bishop Arius (in opposition to Arius). Around 325–353 AD. This Arian text was originally written by Constantius II, who could not bare that the strong Christianity of Athanasius should overshadow his own religion in the empire. It was therefore decided that the writings of Arius should be disproved by an Arian author. In other words, by re-writing Arius' 31 letters, which had already been disproved (although some may not agree with this view), this text would then condemn itself and then become dis-accepted as a heterodox Arian creed (e.g. the text had to contain a great deal of contradictions), when these writings contradicted themselves. Thus, this text was conceived. It is apparent, for instance in letters 1 and 2, that the words were torn from their original context in the writing process. But the so-called Arius text [in a form very close to the above] can be found in book 5 of Eusebius. The text can be viewed as "para-historical" in the way that it is written, since it omits the knowledge Arius had at a later time. On the other hand, the text does state the philosophy of Arius (this text is also detailed in the treatise against Arians, as we know it from the 39th letter of Athanasius). Arius had previously been sent into exile (to Nicomedia), and was recalled due to the visit of Constantius in Egypt. Eusebius of Nicomedia was of course a convinced Arian. This attitude of these authors is very clear, and it appears that they were concerned to write down the real philosophy of Arius. And, in fact, the text contains most of the doctrines which we have learnt from Arius' writings. In other words, although this "Arian" text was read as a diatribe against Arius, the meaning of it was the exact opposite: it gave Arian doctrine a great credibility.

Implications:
- Athanasius' first exile was due to a theological difference with Arsenius, who was opposed to his views on "eisophoria" and "coming back to God" (5th paragraph).
- Constantine had been in contact with Arius, and had attempted to reconcile Athanasius and Arius, despite Athanasius' alleged lack of interest (5th paragraph).
- Arius returned to his position as a bishop, and attracted followers in Rome and Milan (6th paragraph).
- Athanasius falsely accused Arius of theft and misappropriation of church property, leading to Arius' exile (7th paragraph).
- Constantine sent for Arius from exile to his palace at Loodein, where Arius repented and sought Athanasius' forgiveness (8th paragraph).
- Arius and Athanasius broke fellowship due to Arius' secret conversations with Emperor Constantine, despite Athanasius' forgiveness (9th paragraph).
- The text "Arian text" is in fact an Arian author's commentary on the teachings of Arius (10th paragraph).
- The "Arian text" was written by Constantius II in an effort to discredit Athanasius by disproving the writings of Arius, despite their original context and philosophical meaning (10th paragraph).

